Chapter: DtlsMessage
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Hmac
  cites Foreword chapter Hkdf
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256

 The DTLS 1.3 handshake message layer (RFC 9147 sections 5.2 and 7).
 Phase D2b: the framing the D2a machine sequences but does not encode.

 Three things live here, and the first one is the reason the chapter
 exists at all.

 THE TRANSCRIPT RULE. RFC 9147 section 5.2, and it is worth quoting
 because getting it wrong produces a Finished that verifies against
 itself and against no peer on earth: the message transcript is computed
 over the original TLS 1.3-style Handshake messages WITHOUT the
 message_seq, fragment_offset, and fragment_length values. So a DTLS
 handshake message goes on the wire with a 12-byte header and into the
 transcript with a 4-byte one. Two shapes of the same message, and an
 implementation that hashes what it sent is wrong. dtls-msg-transcript
 is the only correct way to feed a message to the hash; the wire form
 must never reach it.

 THE FINISHED. RFC 8446 section 4.4.4, inherited whole. The finished key
 is HKDF-Expand-Label(base-secret, "finished", "", 32) -- an EMPTY
 context, not the transcript -- and the verify data is an HMAC of that
 key over the transcript hash. The two are easy to transpose and the
 result looks like a key either way.

 THE ACK. RFC 9147 section 7. A list of record numbers, each an epoch
 and a sequence number, saying what actually arrived. It is what lets a
 flight stop being retransmitted.

 What is NOT here, stated rather than implied: fragmentation REASSEMBLY.
 The header carries fragment_offset and fragment_length and the parser
 reads them, but this chapter emits only unfragmented messages and does
 not reassemble a fragmented peer flight. A handshake message larger
 than the path MTU will therefore fail against a peer that fragments.
 That is a real gap, it is in BACKLOG 5.3, and it must not be described
 as working.

 Nor is there any peer authentication. A Finished that verifies proves
 the peer holds the same handshake secret; it says nothing about who the
 peer is. The credential question is still open.

 To dtls-msg-encode gives List Integer,
   framing one handshake message with the 12-byte DTLS header, unfragmented.

 To dtls-msg-transcript gives List Integer,
   rendering a message in the TLS 1.3 form the transcript hash must see.

 To dtls-verify-data gives List Integer,
   computing the Finished verify_data from a base secret and a transcript hash.

 To dtls-ack-encode gives List Integer,
   encoding an ACK over the record numbers that arrived.

 We say:

Section: Handshake Types

 RFC 9147 section 5.2. HelloRetryRequest is not in this list: DTLS 1.3
 reuses TLS 1.3's, which is a ServerHello carrying a special random and
 the cookie extension -- not the separate HelloVerifyRequest that DTLS
 1.2 had. A parser that looks for a distinct HRR type will never find one.

  dtls-hs-client-hello : Integer = 1
  dtls-hs-server-hello : Integer = 2
  dtls-hs-encrypted-ext : Integer = 8
  dtls-hs-certificate : Integer = 11
  dtls-hs-cert-verify : Integer = 15
  dtls-hs-finished : Integer = 20

 RFC 9147 section 7: the ACK is its own record content type, 26.

  dtls-content-ack : Integer = 26

Section: Types

  DtlsMsg = record {
    dm-type : Integer,
    dm-length : Integer,
    dm-seq : Integer,
    dm-frag-offset : Integer,
    dm-frag-length : Integer,
    dm-body : List Integer
  }

  DtlsRecordNum = record {
    rn-epoch : Integer,
    rn-seq : Integer
  }

Section: Framing

 The wire form: msg_type, uint24 length, uint16 message_seq, uint24
 fragment_offset, uint24 fragment_length, body. Twelve bytes of header.
 This chapter emits whole messages, so fragment_offset is 0 and
 fragment_length equals length -- see the reassembly gap above.

  dtls-msg-encode : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-encode (msg-type) (message-seq) (body) =
    let n = list-length body
    in [msg-type] & dtls-be24 n & dtls-be16 message-seq & dtls-be24 0 & dtls-be24 n & body

 The transcript form. RFC 9147 section 5.2: no message_seq, no
 fragment_offset, no fragment_length. This is the ONLY thing that may be
 fed to the handshake hash.

  dtls-msg-transcript : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-transcript (msg-type) (body) =
    [msg-type] & dtls-be24 (list-length body) & body

 And the same rule applied to a message that arrived: strip the DTLS
 fields back off before hashing what the peer sent.

  dtls-msg-to-transcript : DtlsMsg -> List Integer
  dtls-msg-to-transcript (m) = dtls-msg-transcript (m.dm-type) (m.dm-body)

  dtls-msg-parse : List Integer -> Maybe DtlsMsg
  dtls-msg-parse (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 12 then None
    else dtls-msg-parse-body bs

  dtls-msg-parse-body : List Integer -> Maybe DtlsMsg
  dtls-msg-parse-body (bs) =
    let len = dtls-rd24 bs 1
    in let seq = dtls-rd16 bs 4
    in let foff = dtls-rd24 bs 6
    in let flen = dtls-rd24 bs 9
    in if 12 + flen > list-length bs then None
    else Just (DtlsMsg { dm-type = list-at bs 0, dm-length = len, dm-seq = seq, dm-frag-offset = foff, dm-frag-length = flen, dm-body = dtls-slice-at bs 12 flen })

Section: Finished

 RFC 8446 section 4.4.4, which DTLS 1.3 inherits unchanged.

   finished_key = HKDF-Expand-Label(BaseKey, "finished", "", Hash.length)
   verify_data  = HMAC(finished_key, Transcript-Hash(...))

 The context on the finished key is EMPTY. The transcript hash is the
 HMAC message, not the key's context. Transposing them yields 32 bytes
 that look exactly as much like a key, and a peer that rejects them
 tells you nothing about which of the two you got wrong. The gate on
 this is RFC 8448's published finished_key.

  dtls-finished-key : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-finished-key (base-secret) = tls13-expand-label base-secret "finished" [] 32

  dtls-verify-data : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-verify-data (base-secret) (transcript-hash) =
    hkdf-words-to-bytes (hmac-sha256 (dtls-finished-key base-secret) transcript-hash)

 The Finished as a framed handshake message, ready for the record layer.

  dtls-finished-msg : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-finished-msg (base-secret) (transcript-hash) (message-seq) =
    dtls-msg-encode dtls-hs-finished message-seq (dtls-verify-data base-secret transcript-hash)

 Verification is constant-time. A Finished check that returns on the
 first differing byte leaks how much of a forgery was right.

  dtls-finished-ok : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-finished-ok (base-secret) (transcript-hash) (offered) =
    dtls-ct-equal (dtls-verify-data base-secret transcript-hash) offered

  dtls-ct-equal : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-ct-equal (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else dtls-ct-loop a b 0 (list-length a) 0

  dtls-ct-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-ct-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) (diff) =
    if i >= n then diff == 0
    else dtls-ct-loop a b (i + 1) n (bit-or diff (bit-xor (list-at a i) (list-at b i)))

Section: The Transcript Hash

 Messages accumulate into the hash in transcript form. The accumulator is
 the running concatenation, hashed on demand -- SHA-256 here has no
 incremental interface, so the handshake transcript is held whole. It is
 a few kilobytes for a real handshake, and it is the one multi-KB
 transient in the whole DTLS stack.

  dtls-transcript-add : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-transcript-add (acc) (msg-type) (body) = acc & dtls-msg-transcript msg-type body

  dtls-transcript-hash : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-transcript-hash (acc) = hkdf-words-to-bytes (sha256 acc)

 THE HELLORETRYREQUEST TRANSCRIPT. RFC 8446 section 4.4.1, and it is the
 third of this stack's silent-interop traps. When a HelloRetryRequest
 happens, ClientHello1 does NOT stay in the transcript. It is REPLACED by
 a synthetic message: handshake type 254 (message_hash), a uint24 length
 of 32, and the hash of ClientHello1. The transcript then continues with
 the HelloRetryRequest and the second ClientHello.

 Miss this and the Finished computes correctly on the no-cookie path and
 wrongly on the cookie path -- so a test that never exercises a
 HelloRetryRequest passes, ships, and then fails against every server
 that does a cookie exchange, which per RFC 9147 is every server that
 cares about denial of service. The bug hides exactly where the security
 feature lives.

  dtls-msg-message-hash : Integer = 254

  dtls-transcript-after-hrr : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-transcript-after-hrr (ch1-transcript) =
    dtls-transcript-after-hrr-hash (dtls-transcript-hash ch1-transcript)

 A stateless server does not HAVE ClientHello1 by the time it needs this
 -- keeping it is precisely the state the cookie exists to avoid. RFC 8446
 section 4.2.2 gives the answer: the server puts the ClientHello1 hash
 INSIDE the cookie and gets it back with the second ClientHello. So the
 hash, not the message, is the thing this takes.

  dtls-transcript-after-hrr-hash : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-transcript-after-hrr-hash (ch1-hash) =
    [dtls-msg-message-hash] & dtls-be24 32 & ch1-hash

Section: ACK

 RFC 9147 section 7. A vector of record numbers, each a uint64 epoch and
 a uint64 sequence number, preceded by its length in BYTES. The ACK says
 what arrived, which is what lets the peer stop retransmitting a flight
 it has already delivered.

  dtls-ack-encode : List DtlsRecordNum -> List Integer
  dtls-ack-encode (rns) =
    let body = dtls-ack-body rns 0 (list-length rns) []
    in dtls-be16 (list-length body) & body

  dtls-ack-body : List DtlsRecordNum, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ack-body (rns) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let r = list-at rns i
    in dtls-ack-body rns (i + 1) n (acc & dtls-be64 (r.rn-epoch) & dtls-be64 (r.rn-seq))

  dtls-ack-count : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-ack-count (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 2 then 0
    else dtls-rd16 bs 0 / 16

Section: Helpers

  dtls-be16 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be16 (v) = [bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-be24 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be24 (v) = [bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-be64 : Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-be64 (v) =
    [bit-and (bit-shru v 56) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 48) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 40) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 32) 255,
     bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  dtls-rd16 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-rd16 (bs) (off) = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bs off) 8) (list-at bs (off + 1))

  dtls-rd24 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-rd24 (bs) (off) =
    let a = bit-shl (list-at bs off) 16
    in let b = bit-shl (list-at bs (off + 1)) 8
    in bit-or a (bit-or b (list-at bs (off + 2)))

  dtls-slice-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-slice-at (bs) (off) (n) = dtls-slice-loop bs off n 0 []

  dtls-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-slice-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else dtls-slice-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))
